   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( y = f(x) \\) be the particular solution to the differential equation \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{x}{y} \\) with \\( f(4) = -2 \\). The largest open interval containing \\( x = 4 \\) on which the solution exists has exactly one finite endpoint. Find that endpoint."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Multiply",
         2,
         [
            "Sqrt",
            3
         ]
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Separate and antidifferentiate: \\( \\frac{y^{2}}{2} = \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + C \\), so \\( y^{2} = x^{2} + 2C \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Power",
            "x",
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At \\( (4, -2) \\): \\( 2C = -12 \\), and the negative root applies, so \\( y = -\\sqrt{x^{2} - 12} \\).",
         "mathjson": -12
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The expression needs \\( x^{2} > 12 \\), because at \\( x^{2} = 12 \\) \\( y = 0 \\) and \\( \\frac{dy}{dx} \\) is undefined.",
         "mathjson": 12
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The piece containing \\( x = 4 \\) is \\( \\left(2 \\sqrt{3}, \\infty\\right) \\), so the finite endpoint is \\( 2 \\sqrt{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            2,
            [
               "Sqrt",
               3
            ]
         ]
      }
   ],
